perations","block":444,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000154","form":"10-Q","filedAt":"2026-07-30","reportDate":"2026-06-30","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000154/xel-20260630.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e.bin","sha256":"702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e"}},{"id":"49e86530033b3deac6f42189cbc10262b355047ba7aada0333c1ca3c6d1edd60","quote":"Earnings from Equity Method Investments — Earnings from equity method investments increased $84 million for the second quarter of 2026 and $98 million year-to-date. The year-to-date change was primarily due to unrealized gains on investment funds’ interests in energy technology companies in the first six months of 2026 and losses in the first six months of 2025.","context":"Interest Charges — Interest charges increased $94 million for the second quarter of 2026 and $174 million year-to-date. The year-to-date change was primarily due to higher debt levels.","section":"Results of Operations","block":445,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000154","form":"10-Q","filedAt":"2026-07-30","reportDate":"2026-06-30","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000154/xel-20260630.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e.bin","sha256":"702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e"}},{"id":"fddf431780eb52c6ea861cefb1a44b623fd856df8e6e789c34611abfe44e48f9","quote":"AFUDC, Equity and Debt — AFUDC increased $54 million for the second quarter of 2026 and $115 million year-to-date. The year-to-date change was primarily due to system investment.","context":"Earnings from Equity Method Investments — Earnings from equity method investments increased $84 million for the second quarter of 2026 and $98 million year-to-date. The year-to-date change was primarily due to unrealized gains on investment funds’ interests in energy technology companies in the first six months of 2026 and losses in the first six months of 2025.","section":"Results of Operations","block":446,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000154","form":"10-Q","filedAt":"2026-07-30","reportDate":"2026-06-30","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000154/xel-20260630.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e.bin","sha256":"702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e"}},{"id":"2ec9d5e8f12db9b5e53939a3bff742807002eb0efb9b13b87042525f35eb5d7c","quote":"The FERC and various state and local regulatory commissions regulate Xcel Energy Inc.’s utility subsidiaries and West Gas Interstate. Xcel Energy is subject to rate regulation by state utility regulatory agencies, which have jurisdiction with respect to the rates of electric and natural gas distribution companies in Minnesota, North Dakota, South Dakota, Wisconsin, Michigan, Colorado, New Mexico and Texas.","context":"Public Utility Regulation and Other","section":"Results of Operations","block":449,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000154","form":"10-Q","filedAt":"2026-07-30","reportDate":"2026-06-30","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000154/xel-20260630.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e.bin","sha256":"702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e"}},{"id":"4af2aaac9a5f9930098a1a767d8631d7e8bca51cf4a747c5c0960a7a3813d0da","quote":"Rates are designed to recover plant investment, operating costs and an allowed return on investment. Our utility subsidiaries request changes in utility rates through commission filings. Changes in operating costs can affect Xcel Energy’s financial results, depending on the timing of rate cases and implementation of final rates. Other factors affecting rate filings are new investments, sales, conservation and demand side management efforts and the cost of capital.","context":"The FERC and various state and local regulatory commissions regulate Xcel Energy Inc.’s utility subsidiaries and West Gas Interstate. Xcel Energy is subject to rate regulation by state utility regulatory agencies, which have jurisdiction with respect to the rates of electric and natural gas distribution companies in Minnesota, North Dakota, South Dakota, Wisconsin, Michigan, Colorado, New Mexico and Texas.","section":"Results of Operations","block":450,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000154","form":"10-Q","filedAt":"2026-07-30","reportDate":"2026-06-30","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000154/xel-20260630.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e.bin","sha256":"702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e"}},{"id":"efab05932d220d14107f8125aa878f951326907a66f58fccd65b40e8c709bc85","quote":"Except to the extent noted below, the circumstances set forth in Public Utility Regulation included in Item 7 of Xcel Energy’s Annual Report on Form 10-K for the year ended Dec. 31, 2025 appropriately represent, in all material respects, the current status of public utility regulation and are incorporated herein by reference.","context":"In addition, the regulatory commissions authorize the ROE, capital structure and depreciation rates in rate proceedings. Decisions by these regulators can significantly impact Xcel Energy’s results of operations.","section":"In addition, the regulatory commissions authorize the ROE, capital structure and depreciation rates in rate proceedings. Decisions by these regulators can significantly impact Xcel Energy’s results of operations.","block":452,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000154","form":"10-Q","filedAt":"2026-07-30","reportDate":"2026-06-30","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000154/xel-20260630.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e.bin","sha256":"702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e"}},{"id":"06de50e959732f617d2a24979a911895fc0dcf980c5b963bfa0982435a7904ba","quote":"Pending and Recently Concluded Regulatory Proceedings","context":"NSP-Minnesota","section":"In addition, the regulatory commissions authorize the ROE, capital structure and depreciation rates in rate proceedings. Decisions by these regulators can significantly impact Xcel Energy’s results of operations.","block":454,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000154","form":"10-Q","filedAt":"2026-07-30","reportDate":"2026-06-30","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000154/xel-20260630.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e.bin","sha256":"702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e"}},{"id":"40a4bc118e3cc1b840090eb40e0d411dc7eecadd16b9b4dd078466bab7542e78","quote":"2024 Minnesota Electric Rate Case — In November 2024, NSP-Minnesota filed an electric rate case in Minnesota based on an ROE of 10.3%, a 52.5% equity ratio and rate base of $13.2 billion in 2025 and $14 billion in 2026. In December 2024, the MPUC approved interim rates of $192 million, effective Jan. 1, 2025. In October 2025, NSP-Minnesota filed rebuttal testimony, updating its total revenue request to $365 million.","context":"Pending and Recently Concluded Regulatory Proceedings","section":"In addition, the regulatory commissions authorize the ROE, capital structure and depreciation rates in rate proceedings. Decisions by these regulators can significantly impact Xcel Energy’s results of operations.","block":455,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000154","form":"10-Q","filedAt":"2026-07-30","reportDate":"2026-06-30","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000154/xel-20260630.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e.bin","sha256":"702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e"}},{"id":"ffa3931e912aa5b32f5ced6b2a5011d7e0aee8fb8a0b0a041d51181f2394c27b","quote":"In June 2026, the MPUC issued a verbal decision. Terms of the decision include:","context":"2024 Minnesota Electric Rate Case — In November 2024, NSP-Minnesota filed an electric rate case in Minnesota based on an ROE of 10.3%, a 52.5% equity ratio and rate base of $13.2 billion in 2025 and $14 billion in 2026. In December 2024, the MPUC approved interim rates of $192 million, effective Jan. 1, 2025. In October 2025, NSP-Minnesota filed rebuttal testimony, updating its total revenue request to $365 million.","section":"In addition, the regulatory commissions authorize the ROE, capital structure and depreciation rates in rate proceedings. Decisions by these regulators can significantly impact Xcel Energy’s results of operations.","block":456,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000154","form":"10-Q","filedAt":"2026-07-30","reportDate":"2026-06-30","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000154/xel-20260630.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e.bin","sha256":"702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e"}}],"omittedEvidenceCount":160},"materialEvents":{"status":"evidence_available","evidence":[{"id":"61a93469ce389e04d5bdd872200b1473f823e02503bd82990be723d143abdddb","quote":"Additionally, in March 2026, NSP-Minnesota, PSCo and SPS each entered into an uncommitted letter of credit agreement with overall limits of $50 million, $50 million and $150 million, respectively, to provide additional letter of credit capacity outside of the revolving credit facilities. As of June 30, 2026, a total of $2 million of letters of credit were outstanding under these continuing letter of credit agreements.","context":"Letters of Credit — Xcel Energy Inc. and its utility subsidiaries use letters of credit, generally with terms of one year, to provide financial guarantees for certain obligations. There were $92 million of letters of credit outstanding under the credit facilities at both June 30, 2026 and Dec. 31, 2025. Amounts approximate their fair value and are subject to fees.","section":"ITEM 1 — FINANCIAL STATEMENTS","block":134,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000154","form":"10-Q","filedAt":"2026-07-30","reportDate":"2026-06-30","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000154/xel-20260630.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e.bin","sha256":"702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e"}},{"id":"3e9258ad2f20128ba46261bccb7c761b89d0d56796d5c3ebc6928894d7f865e0","quote":"Term Loan Agreement — In January 2026, Xcel Energy Inc. entered into a $1.5 billion, 364-Day Delayed Draw Term Loan Agreement. The loan is unsecured and matures Jan. 30, 2027. The term loan includes one financial covenant, requiring Xcel Energy’s consolidated funded debt to total capitalization ratio to be less than or equal to 70 percent. Interest is at a rate equal to the Term SOFR rate, plus 85.0 basis points, or an alternate base rate. As of June 30, 2026 there was $1.5 billion outstanding under the term loan facility.","context":"As of June 30, 2026 and Dec. 31, 2025, NSP-Minnesota had $69 million of outstanding letters of credit under the $75 million bilateral credit agreement.","section":"ITEM 1 — FINANCIAL STATEMENTS","block":137,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000154","form":"10-Q","filedAt":"2026-07-30","reportDate":"2026-06-30","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000154/xel-20260630.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e.bin","sha256":"702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e"}},{"id":"11500943e03ad8ba57d0d8a8aa8da104baa60dca9e3852a96db35b5aaaf52646","quote":"Forward Sale Agreements — Under these ATM programs, Xcel Energy Inc. has entered into multiple forward sale agreements in 2026 and 2025 in connection with completed public offerings of Xcel Energy common stock.","context":"ATM Equity Offerings — Xcel Energy Inc. filed prospectus supplements in August 2025 and May 2026 under which it may sell up to $4 billion and $4.3 billion of its common stock, respectively, through ATM programs. In addition to the issuance and sale of shares of common stock to or through sales agents, Xcel Energy Inc. also may use these ATM programs to enter into forward sale agreements. As of April 30, 2026, no further transactions will occur under the 2025 ATM program.","section":"ITEM 1 — FINANCIAL STATEMENTS","block":146,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000154","form":"10-Q","filedAt":"2026-07-30","reportDate":"2026-06-30","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000154/xel-20260630.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e.bin","sha256":"702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e"}},{"id":"a9ab3a023ab55c6856e6a7a1437f09423b0a73517163aadafca87cedb47cc441","quote":"Stock equivalent units granted to Xcel Energy Inc.’s Board of Directors are included in common shares outstanding upon grant date as there is no further service, performance or market condition associated with these awards. Restricted stock issued to employees is included in common shares outstanding when granted.","context":"Common Stock Equivalents — Common stock equivalents include commitments to issue common stock related to forward equity agreements, collared forward equity agreements and time-based equity compensation awards. To the extent dilutive, these items are included in diluted shares outstanding using the treasury stock method.","section":"ITEM 1 — FINANCIAL STATEMENTS","block":181,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000154","form":"10-Q","filedAt":"2026-07-30","reportDate":"2026-06-30","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000154/xel-20260630.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e.bin","sha256":"702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e"}},{"id":"d3767568c2a5fe63228e9718ee537a5d001962ca3790a29d13fa736730f90c11","quote":"Results of derivative instrument transactions entered into for trading purposes are presented in the consolidated statements of income as electric revenues, net of any sharing with customers. These activities are not intended to mitigate commodity price risk associated with regulated electric and natural gas operations. Sharing of these margins is determined through state regulatory proceedings as well as the operation of the FERC-approved joint operating agreement.","context":"Wholesale and Commodity Trading — Xcel Energy Inc.’s utility subsidiaries conduct various wholesale and commodity trading activities, including the purchase and sale of electric capacity, energy, energy-related instruments and natural gas-related instruments, including derivatives. Xcel Energy is allowed to conduct these activities within guidelines and limitations as approved by its risk management committee, comprised of management personnel not directly involved in the activities governed by this policy.","section":"ITEM 1 — FINANCIAL STATEMENTS","block":228,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000154","form":"10-Q","filedAt":"2026-07-30","reportDate":"2026-06-30","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000154/xel-20260630.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e.bin","sha256":"702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e"}},{"id":"77e989cdc935280a7790f13d7dc46112032101eab988c6396d56a75a64fccafa","quote":"In December 2025, the Texas Attorney General’s office filed a lawsuit against SPS regarding the Smokehouse Creek Fire, seeking monetary damages and civil penalties for losses to property and wildlife resulting from the fires. In February 2026, pending resolution of the lawsuit, SPS and the Texas Attorney General’s office jointly filed a temporary injunction agreeing to certain distribution pole replacement procedures, largely consistent with current procedures.","context":"SPS has received 304 claims through its claims process, net of duplicative, withdrawn and denied claims, and has reached final settlements on 237 of those claims as of the date of this filing. In addition to filed complaints and claims made through SPS’ claims process, SPS has also received information from attorneys for approximately 107 additional claims and has reached settlement of 79 of those claims through mediation.","section":"ITEM 1 — FINANCIAL STATEMENTS","block":296,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000154","form":"10-Q","filedAt":"2026-07-30","reportDate":"2026-06-30","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000154/xel-20260630.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e.bin","sha256":"702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e"}},{"id":"0ab6246e9a7e82f07062cce616d84f125c4f8aef4d3293d32a1b297128bf1deb","quote":"Large Load Agreement — In the first quarter of 2026, NSP-Minnesota entered into an electric service agreement to power a new Google data center in Minnesota. Under the agreement, Google will pay all costs for its new service for the duration of the contract, in accordance with Minnesota’s regulatory and legislative requirements for large loads. If approved, the agreement is expected to result in approximately $1.1 billion of benefits to NSP-Minnesota’s customers. A request for approval of the electric service agreement, including a proposed Clean Energy Accelerator Charge for 1,900 MW of clean energy resources, was filed with the MPUC in April 2026. A decision is expected in early 2027.","context":"•NSP-Minnesota and NSP-Wisconsin may continue to file additional RFPs throughout 2026 and 2027 for resource needs as part of its Upper Midwest resource planning efforts.","section":"In addition, the regulatory commissions authorize the ROE, capital structure and depreciation rates in rate proceedings. Decisions by these regulators can significantly impact Xcel Energy’s results of operations.","block":475,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000154","form":"10-Q","filedAt":"2026-07-30","reportDate":"2026-06-30","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000154/xel-20260630.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e.bin","sha256":"702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e"}},{"id":"9e4d2943bd5c259ea9a64d1974dd10b95c9df75626310917ecf4b310883ed46a","quote":"Term Loan Agreement — In January 2026, Xcel Energy Inc. entered into a $1.5 billion, 364-Day Delayed Draw Term Loan Agreement and as of July 28, 2026 had borrowed $1.5 billion under the term loan facility.","context":"(b)Includes outstanding commercial paper and letters of credit.","section":"In addition, the regulatory commissions authorize the ROE, capital structure and depreciation rates in rate proceedings. Decisions by these regulators can significantly impact Xcel Energy’s results of operations.","block":600,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000154","form":"10-Q","filedAt":"2026-07-30","reportDate":"2026-06-30","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000154/xel-20260630.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e.bin","sha256":"702cb120b259f421ef6f87735ac48163d6781f80373e96674cdfacf686b9365e"}}],"omittedEvidenceCount":0},"riskFactors":{"status":"no_evidence","evidence":[],"omittedEvidenceCount":0},"segmentIntelligence":{"status":"evidence_available","evidence":[{"id":"4b004e714ee592627963b7874647abeb3c5aa54e82e5f1e3b8e3cbd3ff7db804","quote":"Disaggregation of Income Statement Expenses — In November 2024, the FASB is